WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:00.909
Make it stick.

00:00:01.010 --> 00:00:05.020
We want to make words,
vocabulary, structures in the

00:00:05.020 --> 00:00:07.340
language stick in our brains.

00:00:07.450 --> 00:00:08.270
That's the goal.

00:00:08.670 --> 00:00:12.140
And I recently read a book
called Make It Stick by Peter C.

00:00:12.140 --> 00:00:15.990
Brown, where he discusses
the effective strategies that

00:00:15.990 --> 00:00:18.409
enable us to make things stick.

00:00:18.999 --> 00:00:22.519
Now, many of the things he talks about
are things that I have in fact talked

00:00:22.519 --> 00:00:28.520
about previously in my videos, and that is
rather than blocked learning, deliberately

00:00:28.520 --> 00:00:32.220
trying to learn something, reading it
over and over again, you know, underlining

00:00:32.220 --> 00:00:37.010
it, highlighting it, trying to sort of
force it into our brains, we are better

00:00:37.010 --> 00:00:42.745
off If we space our learning activities,
study it now, study it two days later,

00:00:42.754 --> 00:00:49.064
study it a week later, interleave it with
other subject matter, or as I do in my

00:00:49.064 --> 00:00:53.910
language learning I will cover some of
the same material using different sources,

00:00:53.919 --> 00:00:57.329
different starter books, learning the
same material from different angles.

00:00:57.419 --> 00:01:01.760
Or, if I want to become familiar with
a period in history, I'm far better

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:05.570
off to read a variety of different
books on that particular period of

00:01:05.570 --> 00:01:10.810
history, rather than trying to somehow
ace or master one particular book.

00:01:11.110 --> 00:01:13.880
And he also refers to active retrieval.

00:01:13.940 --> 00:01:18.720
When we read another book on a particular
subject, we are in fact connecting

00:01:18.730 --> 00:01:23.320
the information we come across in that
book with information that we acquired

00:01:23.390 --> 00:01:27.810
earlier, and which we may not always
be able to retrieve, but seeing similar

00:01:27.810 --> 00:01:31.470
information in a different setting, in a
different book, in a different context,

00:01:31.780 --> 00:01:37.260
helps us then to solidify our knowledge
of this material you know, a lot of

00:01:37.300 --> 00:01:42.980
what is in his book kind of relates to
this Bloom's Pyramid of different ways

00:01:42.980 --> 00:01:48.390
that we interact with knowledge where we
analyze, or we evaluate, or we compare.

00:01:48.570 --> 00:01:52.785
In other words, we have to interact with
the information that we're acquiring.

00:01:52.785 --> 00:01:55.895
We can't just try to
plug it into our brains.

00:01:55.915 --> 00:01:59.135
In Peter Brown's book, he talks
about the importance of testing.

00:01:59.435 --> 00:02:04.125
Testing is a form of forcing us
to retrieve what we have learned.

00:02:04.475 --> 00:02:08.975
If we can retrieve what we have learned,
and if we can space that retrieval

00:02:09.115 --> 00:02:10.915
activity, we're going to learn it better.

00:02:11.275 --> 00:02:12.435
If we try harder...

00:02:12.700 --> 00:02:16.710
To learn it, to understand it, to analyze
it, we're going to learn it better.

00:02:16.910 --> 00:02:20.380
In other words, the richer our
engagement with that information,

00:02:20.670 --> 00:02:22.070
the better we're going to learn.

00:02:22.110 --> 00:02:25.690
So, we need to space our activities,
we need to interact with the

00:02:25.690 --> 00:02:29.610
material in a meaningful way, we
shouldn't try to block learn things.

00:02:29.660 --> 00:02:33.240
He also talks about the sort of
desirable level of difficulty.

00:02:33.350 --> 00:02:35.690
It shouldn't be too easy, in his opinion.

00:02:36.100 --> 00:02:37.680
Because, again, if we are...

00:02:37.975 --> 00:02:42.595
Engaging with material in a way that
forces us to think about it, then that's

00:02:42.605 --> 00:02:46.815
a richer, higher level of interaction,
which is going to make it easier.

00:02:47.160 --> 00:02:51.030
Then to retain that material
in our long term memory.

00:02:51.230 --> 00:02:53.890
Neurons that fire together, wire together.

00:02:54.030 --> 00:02:57.470
Connecting this bit of information with
some other bit of information, it's

00:02:57.470 --> 00:03:02.130
more likely to create a synapse that
we're going to be able to go back to.

00:03:02.390 --> 00:03:04.660
Now, how does all of this
relate to language learning?

00:03:04.780 --> 00:03:08.270
As I was listening, or because I was
listening to the audio book, it struck

00:03:08.270 --> 00:03:12.230
me that a lot of the things that Peter
Brown talks about in Make It Stick

00:03:12.500 --> 00:03:18.160
is exactly what we do if we have a,
basically an input- based approach

00:03:18.200 --> 00:03:23.100
to language learning, or at least an
approach which involves engaging with the

00:03:23.100 --> 00:03:27.610
language rather than trying to instruct
ourselves on how the language works

00:03:27.620 --> 00:03:29.910
through some analysis of the grammar.

00:03:29.920 --> 00:03:35.830
If I am covering essentially the same
range of vocabulary and language structure

00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:41.490
in a variety of different language
contexts, then I am testing myself on

00:03:41.490 --> 00:03:43.424
my ability to understand what I hear.

00:03:43.835 --> 00:03:48.985
If I then try to speak using
whatever words and structures

00:03:48.985 --> 00:03:53.175
have stuck in my brain, trying to,
you know, trot them out when I'm

00:03:53.205 --> 00:03:55.475
speaking, I'm also testing myself.

00:03:55.525 --> 00:04:00.485
In other words, the ultimate test
is my ability to speak to someone.

00:04:00.495 --> 00:04:05.585
Even now in these, uh, tests like IELTS,
they're relying more and more on that face

00:04:05.585 --> 00:04:07.815
to face test conversation with a tester.

00:04:12.315 --> 00:04:16.865
He suggests that people are overly
optimistic in terms of their self

00:04:16.865 --> 00:04:21.145
evaluation, and I'm sure this is true
if someone asks us, where are you in the

00:04:21.145 --> 00:04:25.335
language, you'll perhaps give yourself
credit for more than you can do, but you

00:04:25.365 --> 00:04:29.195
know yourself, when you're speaking, you
know where you can't remember things,

00:04:29.425 --> 00:04:32.855
you know where you can't understand,
we know where we are in the language.

00:04:33.100 --> 00:04:35.490
So that in a sense is a form of testing.

00:04:35.940 --> 00:04:40.220
And if testing is the answer to
successful language instruction or

00:04:40.220 --> 00:04:43.780
language learning, then we wouldn't have
the situation that we have at least in

00:04:43.780 --> 00:04:48.300
the English language school system in
Canada, where we are regularly tested

00:04:48.600 --> 00:04:52.510
and the vast majority of kids graduate
without being able to speak French.

00:04:52.550 --> 00:04:55.960
I'm talking about French instruction
in the English language school system.

00:04:56.110 --> 00:04:58.270
So testing itself is not the answer.

00:04:58.550 --> 00:05:03.515
Rather, the answer is forcing ourselves
to come across different vocabulary

00:05:03.565 --> 00:05:07.455
items, different structures, in
different contexts, rather than

00:05:07.455 --> 00:05:10.025
trying to ace one particular subject.

00:05:10.055 --> 00:05:13.545
So, in other words, we have to
read widely, we have to listen to

00:05:13.545 --> 00:05:17.715
a wide range of content, so that
we're forcing our brains to retrieve

00:05:17.745 --> 00:05:19.485
things according to a pattern that...

00:05:19.730 --> 00:05:23.690
It doesn't have to be dictated by
some spaced repetition system, it's

00:05:23.690 --> 00:05:27.670
sufficient that it is spaced and
therefore these words will show up with

00:05:27.670 --> 00:05:31.650
some frequency in different contexts
that we are listening to and reading.

00:05:32.100 --> 00:05:36.040
And as long as we continue to
engage with the content in this

00:05:36.040 --> 00:05:40.210
way, we are in fact doing what
Peter Brown suggests in his book.

00:05:40.260 --> 00:05:45.580
Peter Brown also refers to the
importance of knowing how people

00:05:45.580 --> 00:05:48.980
who are learning should know
how the learning process works.

00:05:49.010 --> 00:05:52.650
Therefore, they should know some of
these things about the importance of

00:05:52.750 --> 00:05:54.930
interleaving and not block learning.

00:05:54.970 --> 00:06:00.940
They should know that this much vaunted
learning styles is not true, that we

00:06:01.010 --> 00:06:03.370
all essentially learn the same way.

00:06:03.605 --> 00:06:07.545
Whatever our personal preferences
may be, I prefer to read, I prefer to

00:06:07.545 --> 00:06:10.025
listen, the brain learns in the same way.

00:06:10.145 --> 00:06:15.835
And so learning styles is not a concept
that teachers or learners should hang

00:06:15.835 --> 00:06:20.655
their hat on, rather it's this importance
of retrieval practice, spacing the

00:06:20.655 --> 00:06:23.085
retrieval, varying the activities.

00:06:23.145 --> 00:06:25.715
Another concept that he talks
about, which I think is relevant

00:06:25.725 --> 00:06:26.685
to language learning, he says...

00:06:27.080 --> 00:06:29.540
We should try to do things
before they are explained to us.

00:06:29.550 --> 00:06:33.290
So struggle with the language, for
example, before you have the grammar.

00:06:33.290 --> 00:06:36.730
So if I'm going to start into
Finnish, I'll try to figure out

00:06:36.740 --> 00:06:38.500
how does the Finnish language work?

00:06:38.780 --> 00:06:42.870
Where are the verbs and the nouns and
try to struggle a little bit, trying

00:06:42.870 --> 00:06:44.720
to solve the riddle of the language.

00:06:44.905 --> 00:06:49.205
And then go to the grammar explanation
when you are curious and when you

00:06:49.205 --> 00:06:52.375
have already had this experience with
the language, that's a much better

00:06:52.375 --> 00:06:56.375
way than trying to teach you upfront
how the language works when you don't

00:06:56.375 --> 00:06:58.075
have experience with the language.

00:06:58.125 --> 00:06:59.915
I think this is an interesting book.

00:06:59.975 --> 00:07:04.505
By the way, I will leave a PDF summary
of the book in the description box.

00:07:04.695 --> 00:07:08.545
But I think the point that we should
have a better understanding of how

00:07:08.545 --> 00:07:12.485
the brain learns is an important
one and that much of the learning

00:07:12.485 --> 00:07:16.475
activity in schools that's practiced
in schools where there's an emphasis

00:07:16.545 --> 00:07:22.485
on trying to ace material, block learn,
focus on learning this is misguided.

00:07:22.925 --> 00:07:26.305
And we need to have an approach
that's based on more variety,

00:07:26.335 --> 00:07:29.125
interleaving, spacing out our learning.

00:07:29.145 --> 00:07:32.355
All of these things I think
are very relevant, very useful.

00:07:32.395 --> 00:07:34.715
And, uh, so I bring this
book to your attention.

00:07:34.755 --> 00:07:37.855
Uh, you should look it up and
look up relevant and related

00:07:37.855 --> 00:07:38.875
material on the internet.

00:07:38.905 --> 00:07:41.955
I think it'll help you for
your general learning and also

00:07:41.955 --> 00:07:42.895
for your language learning.

